# [R packages]{style="color:darkblue"}

As you already know, R is a [GNU Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU) project with a [GPL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_General_Public_License) license. Because R is freely available and widely used, a large community of users has developed many extensions called ***R packages***.

The basic idea behind packages is simple: if many people need to solve similar problems, it is useful to collect the relevant functions, documentation, examples and data in a reusable unit. When you install a package, you add new functions or datasets to your R installation.

::: callout-important
## What is an R package?

Packages are the basic units of reusable and reproducible R code. They can contain:

- R functions,
- documentation and help pages,
- example datasets,
- tests,
- vignettes or practical tutorials,
- compiled code or external dependencies in some cases.

With RStudio, you can also create your own packages to organise and reuse frequent analyses. This is beyond the scope of this course, but it is important to know that packages are not only something you download: they are also a way of organising your own code.
:::

Packages can be installed from public R repositories. In this course we will mainly refer to two repositories: [**CRAN**](https://cran.r-project.org/) and [**Bioconductor**](https://www.bioconductor.org/).

CRAN stands for *Comprehensive R Archive Network* and contains a large collection of packages that can be used free of charge. You can find the complete list of CRAN packages [by name](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/available_packages_by_name.html) or grouped by broad topic in the [CRAN Task Views](https://cran.r-project.org/web/views/).

As we will see in more detail later in [Lesson R8](r9.html), Bioconductor is a repository focused on bioinformatics and computational biology applications.

::: callout-warning
## Packages can change over time

Packages in CRAN and Bioconductor are reviewed, but they may change, become archived, or stop working after updates in R, RStudio or external software. You can also find packages on GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket or other repositories, but these packages may be less stable or less carefully reviewed.

In this course, try to install packages from CRAN or Bioconductor unless there is a clear reason to do otherwise.
:::

## [Installing and loading R packages]{style="color:darkblue"}

Installing a package and loading a package are different operations:

- `install.packages()` downloads and installs the package in your computer.
- `library()` loads an installed package into the current R session.

You normally install a package only once, but you need to load it again in each new R session before using its functions.

As an example, we could install the package `xlsx`, which can be used to import and export Excel files. However, this package may require Java in some computers, so it is useful as a teaching example of package requirements.

```{r, echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE}
# Install the package from CRAN.
# This only needs to be done once in your computer.
install.packages("xlsx", repos = "https://cran.rstudio.com/")

# CRAN is the default repository in many R installations,
# but it can also be indicated explicitly with repos =.
```

::: callout-caution
## Package requirements

Some packages have additional system requirements outside R or RStudio. For example, packages that read Excel files may depend on Java or other external libraries. If a package fails to install or load, the problem may not be your code: it may be an external dependency.

After testing with students, we found that `xlsx` may require the path of your Java executable. For routine Excel import/export, simpler alternatives such as [`openxlsx`](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/openxlsx/index.html) or [`readxl`](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/readxl/index.html) are often easier to use.
:::

You must load a package before using its functions directly. There are two common ways to load an installed package: `library()` and `require()`.

```{r, error=TRUE}
# Two ways of loading a package:

# 1. library() loads the package or returns an error if it cannot be loaded.
library(xlsx)

# 2. require() tries to load the package and returns TRUE or FALSE.
# It usually gives a warning rather than stopping immediately with an error.
require(xlsx)

# Open the help page for the package, if it is available.
help(xlsx)

# You can also search help pages from the console with:
# ??xlsx

# A common pattern is to install a package only if it is not already available.
# This is useful in personal scripts, but in teaching materials it may be better
# to ask students to install packages explicitly before class.
if(!require(xlsx)){
    install.packages("xlsx")
    library(xlsx)
}

# Optionally, unload the package from memory.
# This is not usually needed in short scripts, but it can be useful
# when testing package conflicts.
detach("package:xlsx", unload=TRUE)
```

::: callout-note
## `library()` versus `require()`

`library()` and `require()` are often used for similar purposes, but they behave differently when a package cannot be loaded.

- `library(pkg)` is usually preferred in scripts because it clearly fails if the package is missing.
- `require(pkg)` returns `TRUE` or `FALSE`, which makes it useful inside conditional statements.

For beginners, a good rule is: use `library()` when you simply want to load a package; use `require()` only when you need to test whether loading succeeded.
:::

In the following example, we try to load a package called `uam`, which is not expected to exist. Compare the behaviour of `library()` and `require()`.

```{r, error=TRUE}
# Intentional error: this package is not installed and probably does not exist.
# library() stops with an error.
library(uam)

# require() gives a warning and returns FALSE, which can be used in an IF statement.
require(uam)
```

