## [Namespace notation: `package::function()`]{style="color:darkblue"}

Sometimes you may want to use a function from a package without loading the entire package with `library()`. You can do this with the `package::function()` syntax.

This is also useful when two different packages contain functions with the same name. In that case, the namespace prefix makes your code explicit.

```{r error=TRUE, eval=FALSE}
# Example: install a small package from CRAN.
# This chunk is not evaluated when rendering because it installs software.
install.packages("getwiki")

# Open the vignette, if available.
vignette("getwiki")

# Use one function from the getwiki package without loading the package.
DNA <- getwiki::search_wiki("DNA")
str(DNA)
DNA$titles
```


The syntax `package::function()` makes the origin of a function explicit. This is useful in scripts, reports and teaching materials, especially when a function name may exist in more than one package.

In the examples below, we use the `ggplot()` function from the `ggplot2` package. Given the complex syntax of this package (see lesson [R5](r5.html)), this syntax does not look very convenient in this case. A complete plot requires several functions, not only `ggplot()`. This means that using `package::function()` only once may not be enough.

::: {#ggplot}
:::

```{r error=TRUE}
# Example using the built-in iris dataset.
# No need to load iris: it is already available as a built-in dataset.

# Option 1: no package loaded and no namespace prefix.
# Intentional error: ggplot() and aes() are not available unless ggplot2 is loaded
# or we use the ggplot2:: prefix.
ggplot(iris, aes(Petal.Length, Petal.Width, colour = Species)) +
    geom_point()

# Option 2: prefix only ggplot().
# Intentional problem: aes() and geom_point() are still not explicitly found.
ggplot2::ggplot(iris, aes(Petal.Length, Petal.Width, colour = Species)) +
    geom_point()

# Option 3: prefix all ggplot2 functions.
# This is explicit and works without library(ggplot2).
ggplot2::ggplot(iris, ggplot2::aes(Petal.Length, Petal.Width, colour = Species)) +
    ggplot2::geom_point()

# Option 4: load the package and then use the functions directly.
library(ggplot2)
ggplot(iris, aes(Petal.Length, Petal.Width, colour = Species)) +
    geom_point()
```

Note that `iris` is a built-in R dataset. As we mentioned in [Lesson R3](r3.html), R has some built-in datasets that can be used as examples for plots or analysis. You can check all of them using the function `data()`.

