## [Vignettes and official documentation]{style="color:darkblue"}

Many CRAN packages include a reference manual, and some also include one or more *vignettes*. A vignette is a practical guide to a package. You can see the installed vignettes with `browseVignettes()` or open a specific vignette with `vignette()`.

```{r, error=TRUE}
# Browse installed vignettes for selected packages.
# Some packages may not have vignettes installed on your system.
browseVignettes("xlsx")
browseVignettes("seqinr")
browseVignettes("ggplot2")
```

Note that in some cases there may be no official vignette at CRAN. However, as R is an open language, you will often find information about packages or functions in websites such as [rdrr.io](https://rdrr.io/), [RDocumentation](https://www.rdocumentation.org/) or [Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/). Try to distinguish official documentation from user-contributed answers.

## [Too many packages]{style="color:darkblue"}

As there are many packages, it sometimes takes time to test different packages for the same analysis. Also, quite often, some packages become obsolete after a new R or RStudio version. Thus, it is also useful to know how to uninstall packages with `remove.packages()`.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
# Remove a package from your R library.
# Do this only if you are sure you do not need it anymore.
remove.packages("xlsx")
```

::: callout-note
## Package "hygiene"

A reproducible script should make clear which packages are required. At the beginning of a script, load the packages you need with `library()`. Avoid loading many packages “just in case”, because this can make scripts slower, less clear and more prone to function-name conflicts.
:::

