## Conditions inside functions

We can include conditions, loops and any other R expressions inside a function. The next example checks whether a number is even or odd.

```{r}
# A simple R function to check whether x is even or odd.
evenOdd <- function(x){
  # The operator %% returns the remainder of a division.
  # If x %% 2 is 0, the number is divisible by 2.
  if(x %% 2 == 0){
    return("even")
  } else {
    return("odd")
  }
}

# Test the function.
evenOdd(4)
evenOdd(3)
```

You may sometimes see one-line `if` statements without curly braces. They can work, but they are easier to misread or break when the code grows. In teaching materials and scripts, curly braces are usually safer.

```{r}
# Same idea, but written without curly braces.
# This is shorter, but it can be riskier for beginners.
evenOdd2 <- function(x){
  if(x %% 2 == 0)
    return("even")
  else
    return("odd")
}

evenOdd2(4)
evenOdd2(3)
evenOdd2(7)
evenOdd2(8)
```


## Inline functions

There is also a quick way to write a function, for small ones or functions that we will use only once, for example when passing it as an argument to another function. In those cases, we can write a short inline function.

```{r}
# Inline function: the body is a single expression.
f <- function(x) x^2 * 4 + x/3

f(4)
f(0)
f(22)
```

