## Functions with multiple arguments

A function can receive more than one input. The next function calculates the area of a rectangle and prints a formatted message.

```{r}
# A simple R function to calculate the area of a rectangle.
area <- function(length, width){
  # Calculate the area.
  area <- length * width

  # Print a formatted message.
  # paste() combines text and values into one character string.
  print(paste("The area of the rectangle is", length, "x", width, "=", area, "cm²"))
}

# Call the function.
area(2, 3)
```

Printing a message is not the same as returning an object. If you want to reuse the result later, it is usually better to return it.

```{r}
# Now we calculate area and perimeter of a rectangle.
Rectangle <- function(length, width){
  area <- length * width
  perimeter <- 2 * (length + width)

  # Create a list containing both results.
  result <- list("Area" = area, "Perimeter" = perimeter)

  # Return the list so it can be stored and reused.
  return(result)
}

Rectangle(2, 3)
```

Like in any R function, you can call the arguments by position or by name. You can also define default values.

```{r}
# Function with default values.
Rectangle <- function(length = 5, width = 4){
  area <- length * width
  return(area)
}

# Case 1: arguments by position.
Rectangle(2, 3)

# Case 2: arguments by name.
# When arguments are named, their order can be changed.
Rectangle(width = 8, length = 4)

# Case 3: default values.
# If no values are provided, length = 5 and width = 4 are used.
Rectangle()
```

